## The copper of Seffarine square

The small Seffarine square is one of the liveliest places in the medina. **Coppersmiths** hammer copper and brass into pots, trays and cauldrons, in a concert of hammer blows. The square is right next to the **Al Quaraouiyine mosque**, founded in the 9th century by Fatima al-Fihri and considered one of the oldest places of learning in the world.

{% tip title="The right time" %}
Come in the morning, when the workshops are in full swing. Stay back so as not to disturb the work, and ask before taking a photo.
{% /tip %}

## Leather and the tanneries

The tanneries of Fez, including the famous **Chouara tannery**, work hides in large stone vats filled with dyes. You do not visit the vats themselves: you view them from the terraces of the leather shops that surround them. A sprig of mint is often offered to soften the smell.

The shops naturally hope for a sale in return: you are under no obligation to buy, but a word of thanks is appreciated.

## Zellige, ceramics and carved wood

- **Zellige**: these mosaics of hand-cut glazed clay tiles adorn fountains, walls and madrasas. The decoration of the **Bou Inania madrasa** and the **Al-Attarine madrasa** are magnificent examples.
- **Ceramics**: Fez is known for its glazed pottery, especially its characteristic blue. Potters' workshops are mostly outside the medina, where some cooperatives can be visited.
- **Cedar wood**: the Nejjarine Museum of Wooden Arts and Crafts, housed in a former fondouk, displays carved doors, chests and ceilings.

## Buying responsibly

- Take time to compare several shops before buying.
- Check the quality: finishing, leather stitching, strength of the glazes.
- For heavy or fragile items, ask whether the craftsman can pack or ship them.
- Be wary of people who spontaneously offer to guide you to a shop: they often earn a commission.

Haggling is part of the custom: read our tips in [Haggling in the souks](/en/guides/haggling-in-the-souks).
